reader='<p>Johan Schot’s work asks how ways of meeting social needs change, and why similar directions of change recur across different socio-technical systems. Technology, institutions, markets, practices and infrastructure are considered together.</p><section id="distinctions"><h2>Three distinctions to keep</h2><p>The multi-level perspective examines interactions between innovation niches, established regimes and wider landscape pressures. These are analytical levels, not management ranks. A regime includes established rules and practices; it is more than the organisations currently delivering a service.</p><p>Deep Transitions extends the question across systems and longer historical periods. A rule can organise one system; a meta-rule is shared across systems; a meta-regime is a shared rule-set. The proposed sustainable Second Deep Transition is an ambition and research proposition, not an assured historical outcome.</p><p>A successful experiment and a changed regime are different achievements. Asking which rules select, fund and retain an innovation can sharpen inquiry, but does not remove questions of power, legitimacy or contested purpose.</p></section><section id="sources"><h2>Selected sources and locators</h2>'
for sid in [bio,guide,pathways,found,mlps,dts,policy]:
    s=next(x for x in d['sources'] if x['id']==sid);reader+='<article><h3><a href="'+E(s['url'])+'">'+E(s['title'])+'</a></h3><p>'+E(s['notes'])+'</p><p class="meta">'+E(s['source_locator'])+'</p></article>'
reader+='</section><section id="atlas"><h2>Inspect the atlas statements</h2><p>'+' · '.join(atlas(n,l) for n,l in [(schot,'Johan Schot'),(geels,'Frank Geels'),(kanger,'Laur Kanger'),(mlp,'Multi-level perspective'),(dt,'Deep Transitions'),(meta,'Meta-rules')])+'</p><p><a href="/#view=map&amp;focus='+dp+'&amp;layer=substantive&amp;depth=constellation&amp;edge=e30_found_meta">Inspect the source-located meta-rule distinction</a>. Graph statements are candidates with evidence locators; no generic influence or priority claim is added.</p></section><section id="application"><h2>Editorial application: why does a pilot stay exceptional?</h2><p>This public-service exercise is an original editorial application, not a case studied by Schot or a claim that his framework is proven in this setting.</p><p>A fictional relational service helps residents, but depends on temporary funding and staff bypassing ordinary rules. Compare two explanations: the surrounding rules prevent the practice becoming ordinary; or the practice works only under unusually favourable conditions.</p><ol><li>Name one rule: a budget condition, eligibility criterion, measure, professional boundary or decision right. How is it enforced? Who can change it?</li><li>Trace whether the same rule also shapes another service. A resemblance is a lead to test, not enough to establish a shared meta-rule.</li><li>Ask who defines success, whose experience is missing and who may challenge the purpose. Improvement inside a team can shift burdens onto residents.</li><li>Change one condition and observe whether the benefit survives without exceptional workarounds. Specify a result that would challenge your explanation.</li></ol><details><summary>Compare a possible response</summary><p>Track the resident’s benefit alongside the effort and exceptions needed to produce it. Test a revised funding or decision rule across two settings before treating it as a general explanation. Retain disagreement about the desired outcome. A historical theory can guide questions; it cannot grant authority to impose the answers.</p></details><p><a href="/systems-thinking/practice/critical-systems-heuristics/">Examine purposes, authority and affected people</a> · <a href="/systems-thinking/claim-lab/">Test a systems claim</a></p></section><section><h2>Previous SysCoI reading</h2>'
for url,label in [('https://stream.syscoi.com/2018/11/22/co-evolutionary-and-multi-level-dynamics-in-transitions-system-innovation-geels/','Co-evolutionary and multi-level dynamics'),('https://stream.syscoi.com/2022/08/12/the-dynamics-of-transitions-in-socio-technical-systems-a-multi-level-analysis-of-the-transition-pathway-from-horse-drawn-carriages-to-automobiles-1860-1930-geels-2006/','From horse-drawn carriages to automobiles'),('https://stream.syscoi.com/2019/06/25/leading-systems-change-nine-strategies-to-transform-the-way-the-world-works-forum-for-the-future/','Leading systems change: nine strategies')]:reader+='<p><a href="'+url+'">'+label+'</a></p>'
